.TH SEEK_BY_JUMP 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
seek_by_jump \- Seek to a position in a file using a jump

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/seek.h>
.IB #include <lib/io.h>
.IB #include <lib/errmsg.h>

.IB void
.IB seek_by_jump(fd_type fd, int64 amount);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBseek_by_jump\fR function seeks to a position in a file using a jump. If the seek operation fails (due to an error like ESPIPE), it falls back to reading the file to achieve the desired seek position.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IB fd
The file descriptor of the file to be seeked.
.TP
.IB amount
The amount to jump in the file. Positive values move forward, negative values move backward.

.SH RETURN VALUE
The function does not return a value.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBseek_by_jump\fR function:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/seek.h>
#include <lib/io.h>
#include <lib/errmsg.h>

int main() {
    fd_type fd = open("/path/to/file", O_RDWR);
    if (fd == -1) {
        perror("open");
        return 1;
    }

    seek_by_jump(fd, 100); // Seek 100 bytes forward
    close(fd);
    return 0;
}
.nef